patches.behavior:
Stateless.

Helpers:
- `whole(x, lo, hi) = Number.isFinite(x) ? Math.min(hi, Math.max(lo, Math.floor(x + 1e-6))) : lo`. The epsilon keeps 1.9999999999999998 from rounding down to 1.
- `roundDecimal(x, d, mode)` returns `x` rounded to exactly `d` decimals as an en-US digit string without grouping, such as `"-1234.50"`. Round the shortest round-trip decimal of `x` (`String(x)`), not its binary value, so 1.005 at 2 decimals gives "1.01". Reference: `new Intl.NumberFormat("en-US", { useGrouping: false, minimumFractionDigits: d, maximumFractionDigits: d, roundingMode }).format(String(x))`, where `roundingMode` is `"halfExpand"` for nearest, `"floor"` for down, and `"ceil"` for up. This needs Intl.NumberFormat v3 (string input and `roundingMode`). Older engines silently ignore `roundingMode`, so feature-detect it and fall back to a decimal-string rounding helper that produces the same digits. Cache formatters by `(d, mode)`.

Each frame:
```
const style = ctx.input<string>("style");
const d = whole(ctx.input<number>("decimals"), 0, 20);
const mode = ctx.input<string>("rounding");
let n = ctx.input<number>("value");
if (style === "percent") n *= 100;
let digits = roundDecimal(n, d, mode);
let unit = style === "percent" ? "%" : "";
if (style === "compact") {
  const units = ["", "K", "M", "B", "T"];
  let k = 0;
  while (k < 4 && Math.abs(n) >= 1000 ** (k + 1)) k++;
  digits = roundDecimal(n / 1000 ** k, d, mode);
  if (k < 4 && Math.abs(Number(digits)) >= 1000) {
    k++;
    digits = roundDecimal(n / 1000 ** k, d, mode);
  }
  unit = units[k];
}
const negative = digits.startsWith("-") && /[1-9]/.test(digits);
let [intPart, fraction = ""] = digits.replace("-", "").split(".");
if (!ctx.input<boolean>("trailingZeros")) fraction = fraction.replace(/0+$/, "");
intPart = intPart.padStart(whole(ctx.input<number>("minimumDigits"), 1, 21), "0");
const [group, decimal] = { commaPeriod: [",", "."], periodComma: [".", ","], spaceComma: [" ", ","] }[ctx.input<string>("separators")];
if (ctx.input<boolean>("groupThousands")) intPart = intPart.replace(/\B(?=(\d{3})+(?!\d))/g, group);
const body = (negative ? "-" : "") + intPart + (fraction ? decimal + fraction : "") + unit;
ctx.output("text", ctx.input<string>("prefix") + body + ctx.input<string>("suffix"));
```

- Examples (other ports at defaults): 1234.5 → "1,235". Decimals 2 → "1,234.50"; with Trailing Zeros off → "1,234.5". Percent on 0.426 → "43%". Compact on 12345 with Decimals 1 → "12.3K". Compact on 999950 with Decimals 1 → "1.0M", because a result that rounds up to 1000 moves to the next unit ("1M" with Trailing Zeros off). −0.4 → "0". Minimum Digits 2 on 7 → "07". Separators Period and Comma with Decimals 2 on 1234.5 → "1.234,50".
- Sign: an ASCII hyphen-minus goes directly before the digits, after Prefix, so Prefix "$" on −5 gives "$-5". A value that rounds to zero never shows a sign.
- Rounding: Nearest rounds half away from zero (2.5 → 3, −2.5 → −3). Down rounds toward negative infinity (−2.1 → −3). Up rounds toward positive infinity (2.1 → 3). Compact rounds the shortened number.
- Compact units: K = 10³, M = 10⁶, B = 10⁹, T = 10¹². Values below 1000 get no unit, and larger values stay in T (2e15 → "2,000T").
- Edge cases: `value` arrives finite through port coercion. The output never contains exponent notation, NaN, or Infinity: `roundDecimal` writes every digit (1e21 → "1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000"). Non-integer Decimals and Minimum Digits round down and clamp to their ranges. Numbers wired into Prefix or Suffix arrive as text.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index; every input may be a loop.
- Frame 0: no special case.
- Muted: outputs Value through the standard number-to-text coercion (up to 6 decimals, no grouping), so a label still shows something.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Sonobe-native (CONVENTIONS.md §18.1); Origami has no equivalent patch.

State per instance: `{ warned: false }`, used only for warn-once logging. `text` and `parts` are whole-loop ports, so the patch evaluates once per frame rather than once per loop index. `token` and `skipEmpty` are ordinary ports; when a loop arrives on either, the patch reads item 0.

Helper: `graphemes(s)` as in textLength (extended grapheme clusters through `Intl.Segmenter`, with a code-point fallback).

Each frame:
```
const texts = ctx.inputItems<string>("text"); // a scalar arrives as one item
const token = ctx.input<string>("token").normalize("NFC");
const skipEmpty = ctx.input<boolean>("skipEmpty");
const parts: string[] = [];
for (const item of texts) {
  const text = item.normalize("NFC");
  const pieces = token === "" ? graphemes(text) : text.split(token);
  for (const piece of pieces) if (!skipEmpty || piece !== "") parts.push(piece);
}
if (parts.length > 10000) {
  parts.length = 10000;
  if (!state.warned) { ctx.services.log("warn", "Split Text made more than 10,000 parts; the rest were dropped."); state.warned = true; }
}
ctx.output("parts", parts); // emitted as a loop
ctx.output("count", parts.length);
```

- Matching: literal and case-sensitive. Token is never a pattern, and escape sequences aren't interpreted (backslash then n is two characters). Matches are found left to right without overlap: "a,,b" gives ["a", "", "b"], and ",a," gives ["", "a", ""].
- Normalization: Text and Token are normalized to NFC first, so a letter with a composed accent matches the same letter with a combining accent. Parts are NFC text.
- Empty cases: when Token isn't found, Parts is [text]. Empty Text with a non-empty Token gives [""] and Count 1, or [] with Skip Empty. An empty Token gives each grapheme as a part: "👋🏽 hi" gives ["👋🏽", " ", "h", "i"], and empty Text gives [] with Count 0.
- Loops: the output length is the number of parts, not the input loop length. A loop on Text is flattened in order: ["a,b", "c"] with Token "," gives ["a", "b", "c"]. An empty loop on Text gives [] and Count 0.
- Edge cases: the 10,000-part cap matches the engine's loop limit (ARCHITECTURE.md §4), and the warning logs once per restart.
- Muted: Parts passes the Text items through unchanged, and Count is their number.
- Frame 0: no special case.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears `warned`. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami labels the output "Output" and has no Count or Skip Empty. Its literal splitting (inferred) matches Sonobe for non-empty tokens. With an empty token Origami is inferred to return the whole text, while Sonobe splits into characters. Origami doesn't document looped Text input; Sonobe flattens it.

Stateless.

Helper: `escapeRegExp(s) = s.replace(/[\\^$.*+?()[\]{}|\/]/g, "\\$&")`, which stays valid with the `u` flag.

Each frame:
```
const text = ctx.input<string>("text").normalize("NFC");
const find = ctx.input<string>("find").normalize("NFC");
const replace = ctx.input<string>("replace");
if (find === "") {
  ctx.output("output", text);
  return;
}
const pattern = new RegExp(escapeRegExp(find), ctx.input<boolean>("caseSensitive") ? "gu" : "giu");
ctx.output("output", text.replace(pattern, () => replace));
```

- Replacement is literal: the function replacer keeps `$&`, `$1`, and `$$` in Replace from being interpreted. Inserted text is never searched again, so replacing "a" with "aa" doesn't repeat.
- Matching: literal, left to right, and non-overlapping. With Case Sensitive off, matching uses Unicode simple case folding and doesn't depend on the locale. Cache compiled regexes by Find and Case Sensitive.
- Normalization: Text and Find are NFC-normalized before matching, so Output is NFC text even when nothing matches.
- Edge cases: an empty Find returns Text unchanged; it never inserts Replace between characters. Empty Text returns "".
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index; all four inputs may be loops.
- Frame 0: no special case.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: same ports and meaning (`builtin.textreplace`). Origami doesn't document the Case Sensitive default or the empty-Find result; Sonobe uses false and returns Text unchanged. Origami's matching is inferred to be literal, which Sonobe makes explicit.

Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const text = ctx.input<string>("text");
const upperFirst = (_match: string, gap: string, punctuation: string, letter: string) => gap + punctuation + letter.toUpperCase();
let output: string;
switch (ctx.input<string>("case")) {
  case "lowercase":
    output = text.toLowerCase();
    break;
  case "capitalize":
    output = text.toLowerCase().replace(/(^|\s)(\p{P}*)(\p{L})/gu, upperFirst);
    break;
  case "sentence":
    output = text.toLowerCase().replace(/(^\s*|[.!?]\s+)(\p{P}*)(\p{L})/gu, upperFirst);
    break;
  default: // "uppercase"
    output = text.toUpperCase();
}
ctx.output("output", output);
```

- Case mapping: `toUpperCase` and `toLowerCase`, never the locale-sensitive variants, so results don't depend on the viewer's language (Turkish i follows the root mapping). Full mappings can change length: "ß" uppercases to "SS".
- Words (Capitalize Words): a word starts at the beginning of the text or after whitespace. Leading punctuation is skipped, so "(hello" becomes "(Hello" and "¿qué" becomes "¿Qué". A word that starts with a digit keeps its letters small ("1st"). Apostrophes and hyphens don't start words: "don't" → "Don't", "self-driving" → "Self-driving". Letters after the first are lowercased: "iPhone" → "Iphone".
- Sentences (Sentence Case): the first letter of the text, and the first letter after ".", "!", or "?" followed by whitespace, become capitals. Everything else is lowercased, including names and "I".
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index.
- Edge cases: empty Text gives "". An unknown enum key behaves as Uppercase.
- Frame 0: no special case.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Text Transform (`builtin.texttransform`). Its Transform options Uppercase, Lowercase, and Capitalized (stored indices 0, 1, 2; order from the docs) import as `uppercase`, `lowercase`, and `capitalize`. If a file stores an index outside that range, import it as `uppercase` and log one warning. Origami doesn't document its word rules; Sonobe capitalizes only after whitespace. Sentence Case has no Origami equivalent.

State per loop index: `{ warned: false }`, used only for warn-once logging (see Caching for optional memoization).

Each frame:
```
const text = ctx.input<string>("text");
const style = {
  fontFamily: ctx.input<string>("fontFamily"),
  fontSize: ctx.input<number>("fontSize"),
  fontWeight: ctx.input<number>("fontWeight"),
  letterSpacing: ctx.input<number>("letterSpacing"),
  lineHeight: ctx.input<number>("lineHeight"),
};
const maxWidth = ctx.input<number>("maxWidth");
const measured = measure(text, style, maxWidth > 0 ? maxWidth : null);
const paragraphs = text.split(/\r\n|\r|\n/).length;
const height = measured.height + Math.max(0, ctx.input<number>("paragraphSpacing")) * (paragraphs - 1);
ctx.output("size", [Math.max(0, finite(measured.width)), Math.max(0, finite(height))]);
```
`finite(x)` returns `x` when finite; otherwise it returns 0 and logs one warning per restart through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`.

- Measuring: `measure` is the runtime's injected `TextMeasurer` (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.4), the same one layout uses for Text layers. A Measure Text with a layer's text and style therefore gives that layer's auto size. Patches reach it through `ctx.services.measureText(text, style, maxWidth)`. Headless runtimes use `approximateTextMeasurer` from `@sonobe/engine`, so expect small differences from DOM-rendered text.
- Layout rules come from the measurer: greedy word wrap at Max Width, words longer than a line break by character, line breaks always start a new line, width is the widest line, and height is lines × line height with at least one line. The headless measurer treats Font Size ≤ 0 as 17 and Line Height 0 as 1.2 × Font Size.
- Timing: results are for the current frame. No layer is involved, so there's no one-frame latency (unlike layer-derived outputs, gap-fill R2).
- Caching: implementations may memoize the last result per loop index keyed on every input. The DOM measurer's results change when a web font finishes loading, so invalidate the cache on font-load events when the host reports them, and otherwise re-measure every frame.
- Edge cases: empty Text gives `[0, one line height]`. Max Width ≤ 0 means no wrapping. Negative Letter Spacing narrows text, but neither component goes below 0.
- Platform and simulation: fonts must be available where the prototype runs; a missing font measures with the fallback font the renderer would use. Headless runs ignore Font and use approximate per-character widths, so golden tests should compare sizes with a tolerance.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index; a loop of labels gives a loop of sizes.
- Muted: outputs `[0, 0]`.
- Frame 0: no special case.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears `warned` and any cache. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Text Size (`builtin.textsize`). Importers map the width of Box Size to Max Width, dropping the box height, which has no equivalent. Font Name maps to Font, and Character Spacing maps to Letter Spacing. Weight takes its default, 400. Like Origami, Sonobe measures on the device running the prototype.

State per loop index: `{ warned: false }`, used only for warn-once logging.

Helpers: `pad(n, width) = String(n).padStart(width, "0")`. `MONTHS` are January to December and `DAYS` are Sunday to Saturday; short names are their first three letters.

Each frame:
```
const t = ctx.input<number>("time");
const format = ctx.input<string>("format");
if (format === "mediaTime" || format === "shortMediaTime") {
  const s = Math.floor(Math.abs(t) + 1e-6);
  const h = Math.floor(s / 3600);
  const m = Math.floor((s % 3600) / 60);
  const sec = s % 60;
  const lead = h > 0 ? h : m;
  const first = format === "mediaTime" ? pad(lead, 2) : String(lead);
  const body = h > 0 ? first + ":" + pad(m, 2) + ":" + pad(sec, 2) : first + ":" + pad(sec, 2);
  ctx.output("text", (t < 0 && s > 0 ? "-" : "") + body);
  return;
}
if (!(t >= -62135596800 && t < 253402300800)) { // years 1 to 9999
  if (!state.warned) { ctx.services.log("warn", "Format Date & Time: Time is outside years 1 to 9999."); state.warned = true; }
  ctx.output("text", "");
  return;
}
const ms = Math.floor(t * 1000);
const zone = ctx.input<string>("timeZone") === "utc" ? "UTC" : deviceTimeZone();
const p = partsOf(ms, zone);
const pattern = format === "custom" ? ctx.input<string>("customFormat") : PRESETS[format];
ctx.output("text", strftime(pattern, p));
```

- `partsOf(ms, zone)` gives `{ year, month (1–12), day, hour (0–23), minute, second, millisecond, weekday (0–6, Sunday = 0), dayOfYear (1–366), offsetMinutes, zoneName }`. Read the calendar fields with `new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-US", { timeZone: zone, hourCycle: "h23", year: "numeric", month: "numeric", day: "numeric", hour: "numeric", minute: "numeric", second: "numeric", weekday: "short", timeZoneName: "short" }).formatToParts(ms)`, and cache one formatter per zone. `millisecond = ((ms % 1000) + 1000) % 1000`. `offsetMinutes` is (the fields read back as a UTC time − `Math.floor(ms / 1000) * 1000`) / 60000, built with `setUTCFullYear` so years below 100 aren't shifted. `zoneName` is the `timeZoneName` part, such as "UTC", "PST", or "GMT+5:30".
- `deviceTimeZone()`: `ctx.services.device().timeZone`, the device's IANA time zone. Deterministic simulation reports "UTC" unless the host sets a zone, so traces reproduce on every machine. A zone Intl can't format falls back to "UTC" when `ctx.services.deterministic`, else to `Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone`.
- `PRESETS`: time12Hour `"%-I:%M %p"`, time12HourSeconds `"%-I:%M:%S %p"`, time24Hour `"%H:%M"`, time24HourSeconds `"%H:%M:%S"`, shortDateYmd `"%Y-%m-%d"`, shortDateDmy `"%d-%m-%Y"`, shortDateMdy `"%m-%d-%Y"`, mediumDateMdy `"%b %-d, %Y"`, mediumDateDmy `"%-d %b, %Y"`, longDateMdy `"%B %-d, %Y"`, longDateDmy `"%-d %B, %Y"`. For Time 1773513005 in UTC they give 6:30 PM, 6:30:05 PM, 18:30, 18:30:05, 2026-03-14, 14-03-2026, 03-14-2026, Mar 14, 2026, 14 Mar, 2026, March 14, 2026, and 14 March, 2026.
- `strftime(pattern, p)` codes. Numeric codes pad with zeros unless noted, and a `-` flag after `%` removes padding from d, e, H, I, j, k, l, m, M, and S:

| Code | Value | Code | Value |
|---|---|---|---|
| %a | Sat | %A | Saturday |
| %b, %h | Mar | %B | March |
| %C | 20 | %c | same as "%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Y" |
| %d | 01–31 | %D | same as "%m/%d/%y" |
| %e | 1–31, padded with a space | %F | same as "%Y-%m-%d" |
| %H | 00–23 | %I | 01–12 |
| %j | 001–366 | %k | 0–23, padded with a space |
| %l | 1–12, padded with a space | %L | 000–999 milliseconds |
| %m | 01–12 | %M | 00–59 |
| %n | line break | %p | AM or PM |
| %r | same as "%I:%M:%S %p" | %R | same as "%H:%M" |
| %S | 00–59 | %t | tab |
| %T | same as "%H:%M:%S" | %u | 1–7, Monday = 1 |
| %w | 0–6, Sunday = 0 | %x | same as "%m/%d/%y" |
| %X | same as "%H:%M:%S" | %y | 00–99 |
| %Y | at least 4 digits | %z | +hhmm or −hhmm offset (ASCII sign) |
| %Z | zoneName | %% | % |

- Unknown codes stay as typed ("%Q" stays "%Q"), and a lone "%" at the end stays "%". Week-number codes (%U, %V, %W, %G, %g) aren't supported and stay as typed. %L is a Sonobe addition.
- Time: fractional seconds round down to milliseconds with `Math.floor`, so −0.5 is 23:59:59 on December 31, 1969, UTC. Media Time rounds down to whole seconds after a 1e-6 epsilon, so 59.9 shows 00:59. Negative durations show a leading "-" unless they round down to 0.
- Edge cases: Time outside years 1 to 9999 (−62135596800 ≤ t < 253402300800) outputs "" and logs one warning per restart. An empty Custom Format outputs "". An unknown format key uses time12Hour.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index; a loop of timestamps gives a loop of labels.
- Muted: outputs Time through the standard number-to-text coercion.
- Frame 0: no special case. The patch doesn't read a clock itself; wire a time source into Time for a live display.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears `warned`. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Date & Time Formatter (`builtin.time.formatter`), with ports Time, Format, and Custom Format. Its Format enum stores indices 0–13 in the order of `enumOptions` here (order inferred from the reversed option strings in official files), and importers map them to these keys. Origami formats in the device's time zone, so imports use `timeZone: "device"`. Origami's Media Time output for durations over an hour is undocumented.
